<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deccan sultanates</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> sultanates were a group of Muslim kingdoms, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, that emerged in the Deccan after the break-up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> grew out of this environment, as Maratha commanders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle">Shahaji</a> served the sultanates before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> built an independent state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates.jpg" alt="Deccan sultanates" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Beylarbey · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deccan_Sultanates_in_16th_Century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> sultanates is a historians' term for five Persianate Muslim kingdoms on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindhya-range">Vindhya Range</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>. They emerged as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> disintegrated. Ahmadnagar was the first to declare independence, in 1490, followed by Bijapur and Berar in the same year, Bidar around 1492 and Golconda in 1512. Their founders were of varied origins: Ahmadnagar's dynasty descended from Malik Hasan Bahri, a Marathi Muslim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> origin, Berar's founder was a Kannadiga Muslim of Brahmin origin, Bidar's a Georgian slave, and Golconda's a slave of Iranian Turkmen origin. All based their legitimacy on succeeding the Bahmani Sultanate and kept using Bahmani coinage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The sultanates were generally rivals, and factions divided their courts between Deccani Muslims and foreign-born nobles. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, a coup under Jamal Khan led to massacres of foreign nobles in 1588-1591 and the flight of Persian nobles to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, among them the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a>. In 1565 four of the five allied to defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Battle of Talikota</a>, after which the city of Vijayanagara was destroyed and Bijapur took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a>. Ahmadnagar conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> in 1574, and Bijapur annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> in 1619.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> then absorbed them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> was taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> in 1596, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chand-bibi">Chand Bibi</a> repulsed a Mughal attack the same year. After her death in July 1600 the Mughals took Ahmadnagar, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar">Malik Ambar</a> defied them and set up Murtaza Shah II at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paranda">Paranda</a>. Shahaji, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>'s help, put an infant Nizam Shahi scion on the throne, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> annexed the sultanate in 1636 after defeating him. In Bijapur the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> revolted under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> and seized much of the realm. Aurangzeb's campaign of 1686-87 conquered Bijapur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>, ending the last of the sultanates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The courts fostered Dakhani, a language that grew by borrowing from Arabic, Persian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and Telugu, and was later called Dakhani Urdu. Deccani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a> flourished at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>. Architecture of the period includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charminar">Charminar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gol-gumbaz">Gol Gumbaz</a>, and a number of the sultanates' monuments are on a tentative list for UNESCO World Heritage nomination. The Nizam Shahi, Adil Shahi and Qutb Shahi rulers are noted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>; under Ahmad Shah I, Ahmad Shah I's record keeper Dalapati wrote the encyclopaedic Nrisimha Prasada, naming his overlord Nizamsaha.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deccan sultanates” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Barid Shahis then ruled Bidar in their own name, and the Bahmani kingdom was divided among five <b>Deccan sultanates</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its early decades were spent fighting the neighbouring <b>Deccan sultanates</b> of Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar, and the Vijayanagara Empire to the south over the fertile Raichur doab between the Krishna and Tungabhadra rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Maratha Empire arose on the Deccan Plateau in the mid-17th century as a regional power challenging the <b>Deccan sultanates</b> and, in time, the Mughal Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Aurangzeb (r. 1658–1707), the empire reached its greatest territorial extent, annexing the <b>Deccan sultanates</b> of Bijapur in 1686 and Golconda in 1687 and waging decades of war against the Marathas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its long rivalry with the Bahmani Sultanate, and later with the <b>Deccan sultanates</b> that succeeded it, centred on the fertile Raichur Doab between the Krishna and Tungabhadra rivers and began in the reign of Bukka I with notoriously destructive campaigns on both sides.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Barid Shahi state was small, centred on Bidar and its fort, and depended on the balance between the larger <b>Deccan sultanates</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bidar-sultanate">Bidar Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
